## Introduction
The enzyme plays a crucial role in maintaining life-sustaining biological transformations, exhibiting remarkable catalytic selectivity and activity [1,2]. It is promising for ever-expanding applications of enzymes in bioengineering, therapy, environmental protection, biosensing, and so on [3-6]. Nevertheless, the implementation is challenged by the fragile nature of enzymes under unfavorable conditions, which can destroy the unique spatial structure of enzymes or directly poison their active sites [7-9]. To this end, researchers have devoted themselves to the rational design of the “armor-like” nanomaterials  for encapsulation of enzymes to protect them from external stimulus, thus retaining their functionality under extreme environments [10-14]. Besides, benefiting from the functional diversity, a great number of nanomaterials with enzyme-mimicking properties have been used as the substitute for enzymes to circumvent the abovementioned weakness of enzymes [15-17].
Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) constructed by joining metal nodes and organic linkers, have emerged as promising porous materials for immobilizing enzymes [18-22]. The tailorable pore channels, high surface area, and rigid framework can not only provide suitable pore size for loading enzymes to diminish leaching but also shield enzymes from interferences in inhospitable conditions [23-28]. Unfortunately, robust immobilization strategies may change the unique spatial structure of enzymes, decreasing their intrinsic activities. As for surface bioconjugation, large amounts of exposed enzymes on the surface still suffer from external interference [29-31]. Although the internal encapsulation strategy well circumvents the above limits, the tightly confined environment of the cavity (usually $<2~\mathrm{nm}\dot{}$ ）affects the conformational freedom of enzymes, and the communication between internally buried enzymes and substrates is restricted [32-35]. Therefore, the rational design of functional MOFs with appropriate cavity structures to meet the need for spatial distribution and conformation of enzymes is the key point to achieving efficient enzyme immobilization. In addition, engineering MOFs is expected to further enrich the functions of carriers beyond enzyme immobilization.
Herein, two-dimensional (2D) defective Co-based MOFs with “emmentaler"-like structure for immobilizing enzymes were reported, where the ferrocenedicarboxylic acid (Fc) serves as the missing linker modulator to form defect protein trap sites (CoBDC-Fc). As a result, the increase in pore size and volume endows the Fc-engineered protein trap with a higher loading capacity for enzymes than that of pristine CoBDC. Notably, the bioactivity of CoBDC-Fc/enzyme composites is superior to the equivalent free enzymes. Experiment results indicated that the additional Fc endows the CoBDC-Fc with enzyme-like properties to serve as a cocatalyst for synergistic enhancing the enzymatic activities (peroxidase (POD) and catalase (CAT)). Moreover, the Fc not only induces the conformation change of glucose oxidase (GOx) but also acts as the cofactor to promote mass transfer, thus effectively improving the bioactivity of GOx. Owing to the protective effect of MOFs, the immobilized enzymes show good activity in inhospitableenvironments.Inspired by the multifunctional protein trap structure, the antibody of prostatespecific antibody (PSA） and GOx were co-immobilized into CoBDC-Fc to establish a cascade enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) platform for sensitive colorimetric detection of PSA. This work paves a new way for the design and synthesis of functionalized protein traps for efficient enzyme immobilization and mimicking.
