## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Construction of MOFs composites
## 3.2 Construction of MOF/enzyme composites
To evaluate the feasibility of the as-prepared protein traps, four enzymes including Cyt-C, HRP, GOx, and CAT of different sizes were selected as the model proteins (Table Sl in the ESM). Here, four enzymes are respectively immobilized in the MOFs via a postsynthetic packaging strategy (Fig. 2(a)). Upon immobilization, the morphology of MOF/enzyme remains in comparison with pristine MOFs (Fig.2(b) and Figs.S4 and S5 in the ESM). Besides, no observable change in XRD patterns shows that the crystal structure of MOFs was preserved (Fig.2(c) and Fig. S6(a) in the ESM). The additional spectral bands (amide I band, 1,700- $1{,}610\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ）in the Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra of both MOF/enzymes indicate the successful immobilization of enzymes (Fig.2(d) and Fig.S6(b) in the ESM). To visualize the encapsulated enzymes, two typical fluorescent probes, fluorescein isothiocyanate isomer (FITC) and fluorochrome rhodamine B (RhB),were used to label enzymes [38]. Confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) images present obvious green/red fluorescent signals, confirming that enzymes were distributed throughout the MOFs (Fig.2(e) and Figs. S7-S10 in the ESM). Subsequently, the loading amounts of various enzymes were calculated through the fluorescence-labeled and colorimetric methods (Figs. Sll and S12 in the ESM). As summarized in Fig.2(f) and Table S2 in the ESM, the loading capacities of CoBDC-Fc are higher than those of CoBDC and the loading amounts of enzymes show a negative correlation with their sizes. Furthermore, the larger weight loss of CoBDC-Fc/GOx confirms its superior immobilization efficiency to that of CoBDC/GOx through thermogravimetric analysis (Fig. S13 in the ESM).
To investigate whether the enzymes infiltrate into the asprepared protein trap, the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption experiment was first conducted.As shown in Fig.Sl4 inthe ESM, upon immobilization $\mathrm{GOx},$ the surface area and pore volume of CoBDCFc are dramatically reduced. However, the variation of the surface area is higher than that of pore volume for the CoBDC system. This phenomenon suggests that the GOx with a large size ( $\mathrm{\dot{6}.0\ n m}$ $\times5.2\mathrm{nm}\times7.7\mathrm{nm}$ ）may infiltrate into mesoporous $(>5\mathrm{nm})$ and adsorb on the surface in CoBDC-Fc simultaneously. Nevertheless, the pore of CoBDC is not big enough to encapsulate GOx, leading to that the GOx prefers to adsorb on the surface of CoBDC. Then, the recycling experiments of MOF/enzymes show that the leaching amounts of enzymes in CoBDC-Fc/enzymes are lower than that of CoBDC/enzymes when the size of enzymes (e.g., HRP and GOx) is smaller than the pore of CoBDC-Fc but larger than the pore of CoBDC (Fig.S15 in the ESM). According to the previously reported works [24,39],it is believed that small-size enzymes (Cyt-C, HRP, and GOx) prefer to infilter into the defect mesoporous in CoBDC-Fc, which can provide more intermolecular forces to stabilize enzymes (Fig.Sl6(a) in the ESM). Besides, the surface absorption of enzymes is inevitable. As for CoBDC, enzymes tend to absorb on its surface because the CoBDC lacks defective structures. However, when the pore size is smaller than that of the size of enzymes (e.g., CAT), the enzyme prefers to adsorb on the surface of CoBDC-Fc, and the advantages of protein trap are covered (Fig. Sl6(b) in the ESM).
## 3.3 Evaluation of properties of enzymes
To study the effect of MOFs on the performance of enzymes, the bioactivity of enzymes was evaluated systematically before and after immobilization. As shown in Figs. 3(a)-3(c), the catalytic reaction rate and efficiency of CoBDC-Fc/enzymes are superior to those of equivalent free enzymes, while the catalytic activity of CoBDC/enzymes bears a comparison with the free enzymes. On the other hand, both MOF/CAT composites present higher bioactivity than free CAT (Fig.3(d)). Based on these, it is believed that the 2D CoBDC with a thin size can circumvent the influence of mass transfer resistance of three-dimensional (3D) structure MOFs [40], thereby maintaining the catalytic efficiency of enzymes. To uncover the reason for the enhanced catalytic activity, the enzyme-like activity of MOFs was evaluated, which are expected to act as enzyme mimics (named nanozymes) due to the rich metal sites. Interestingly, the CoBDC-Fc expresses PODlike and CAT-like activities (Fig.Sl7 in the ESM),while the CoBDC  only  presents CAT-mimicking catalytic  property. Through a comprehensive comparison of the activity of CoBDCFc, free enzymes, and CoBDC-Fc/enzyme composites, it can be concluded that the enhanced activity of MOF/enzyme (Cyt-C, HRP, and CAT) composites can be attributed to the cocatalyst effect of MOFs, providing the additional enzyme-like activity of MOFs catalytic performance (Fig. 3(e) and Fig. S18 in the ESM).
As a protein trap, protecting the enzyme from the external stimulus is a critical performance. Here, the stability of enzyme in extreme environments was evaluated. As shown in Fig. 4(c) and Fig. S24 in the ESM, the obtained MOF/GOx still possesses bioactivity when exposed to denaturing conditions (such as high temperature, strong acid, alkaline, and organic solvent). In contrast, the activity of free GOx dramatically diminished under the above harsh conditions. Importantly, the tolerability of CoBDC-Fc/GOx is superior to CoBDC/GOx due to the protein trap with stronger interaction forces confining GOx to maintain the conformation against the external stimulus [24]. Furthermore, the recyclability of GOx in different systems was investigated (Fig. 4(d)). The catalytic activity of CoBDC-Fc/GOx retained $89\%$ after four cycles, while the CoBDC/GOx system possesses a $24\%$ reduction in enzymatic activity. Taken together, the Fc-engineered protein trap can not only construct appropriated anchor sites to increase the enzyme immobilization efficiency but also endow the protein trap with additional functions, such as the modulation of enzyme conformation and the introduction of cofactor and cocatalysts, boosting the catalytic efficiency of immobilized systems (Fig. 4(e)). As shown in the radar map, the largest area of CoBDC-Fc reflects the superiority of the protein trap strategy for enzyme immobilization in the aspects of loading amounts, bioactivity, recyclability, tolerance, and stability (Fig. 4(f)).
## 3.4 Construction of biomimetic cascade immunoassay platform
Prostate cancer is a major cancerous disease in men. Rapidly and sensitive monitoring of the disease is great of important [51]. Therefore, by virtue of the protein trap structure, the as-prepared CoBDC-Fc/GOx composites were further applied to immobilize the antibody of PSA for the construction of the ELISA platform (Fig.5(a)). With the increase of PSA concentration, the absorbance at $652\ \mathrm{nm}$ gradually increases and shows a good positive linear relationship in the range of ${5{-}800}\ \mathrm{pg{\cdot}m L^{-1}}$ (Fig. 5(c) and Fig.S33 in the ESM). And the limit of detection is calculated to be $0.52\ \mathrm{pg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the proposed ELISA presents superior sensitivity in comparison with other previous methods (Table Sll in the ESM). The interference experiment verifies the good selectivity of the CoBDC-Fc/GOx-based biosensor (Fig. 5(d)). Furthermore, the PSA concentrations of practical serum samples (two healthy persons and two patients) were monitored. As summarized in Table Sl2 in the ESM, the results are in good agreement with the test result of the hospital. These findings suggested that our proposed biosensor is expected to sensitively and accurately monitor the concentration of PSA in practice.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, a missing-linker strategy was used to simultaneously introduce abundant defects and redox-active Fc sites in CoBDC. Taking advantage of the augmented pore sizes, CoBDC-Fc shows a good ability to anchor enzymes, and the concept of protein trap is firstly proposed for enzyme immobilization. Importantly, the Fcfunctionalized protein trap with enzyme-like activities enables synergistic improvement of the biocatalytic efficiency. Moreover, the hydrophobicity Fc can change the conformation of enzymes and serve as a cofactor to facilitate electron transfer, boosting the catalytic activity of CoDBC-Fc/GOx composites. Protected by the exoskeleton of MOFs, the immobilized enzymes possess excellent stability in inhospitable conditions and remarkable recyclability. Furthermore, benefited from the enhanced cascade catalytic efficiency of $\mathrm{CoBDC-Fc/GOx},$ an additional immobilization of antibody further realized sensitive colorimetric immunoassay of PSA. We believe that the introduction of a protein trap can not only open an avenue for the study of enzyme immobilization but also uncover the veil of the interactions between proteins and nanomaterials.
